1
00:00:01,230 --> 00:00:20,930
Here's how we repointed an existing Power BI report onto a Unity Catalog metric view. /importBI turns the semantic model into metric views, and publish to Power BI turns them back into a semantic model. But the existing report still points at the old model.

2
00:00:21,680 --> 00:00:30,430
This is Report A: seventeen visuals on a star-schema semantic model, running DirectQuery on Databricks SQL.

3
00:00:31,080 --> 00:00:36,629
Model A has eight tables, seven relationships, and four DAX measures.

4
00:00:39,720 --> 00:00:48,070
Step one. In Genie Code, /importBI reads the model's schema: the tables, the measures, and the relationships.

5
00:00:50,790 --> 00:00:58,070
It plans one metric view per fact, and it flags that Order Count changes meaning at line-item grain.

6
00:01:01,270 --> 00:01:06,050
Then it hands back the create view statements, which we ran as is.

7
00:01:17,160 --> 00:01:22,880
Step two. The metric views land in Unity Catalog, with all four measures.

8
00:01:23,470 --> 00:01:32,850
Step three. We publish to Power BI. Fix and publish added the missing order key dimension, so every measure made it across.

9
00:01:33,580 --> 00:01:41,580
Model B has one table per metric view, with the measures translated to DAX. And no relationships.

10
00:01:44,120 --> 00:01:56,870
That's why a plain rebind breaks. Report A asks for fields that Model B doesn't have. So we rewrite the report's own field references, and push it back, bound to Model B.

11
00:01:57,840 --> 00:02:05,460
Step four. The field mapping matches columns on lineage and measures on name, with one manual rule.

12
00:02:06,150 --> 00:02:14,700
The rewrite remaps seventeen of eighteen parts, and reports the one cross-fact visual and the slicer scope changes.

13
00:02:15,460 --> 00:02:25,030
Then a parity check runs the same DAX for every visual through both models. Sixteen of sixteen return identical rows.

14
00:02:25,770 --> 00:02:32,190
And the report was repointed in place: same report ID, now bound to Model B.

15
00:02:32,830 --> 00:02:38,450
Here's the same report, now on the metric views. The totals are identical.

16
00:02:39,250 --> 00:02:49,820
Customer visuals moved to the customer-grain view: one point five million customers, not the nine hundred ninety-nine thousand who placed orders.

17
00:02:50,550 --> 00:02:59,580
Visual and page filters were rewritten too. The one visual that needs two metric views is flagged, not silently wrong.

18
00:03:00,270 --> 00:03:08,240
In lineage, the repointed report now reads the published metric view, while the original still uses Model A.

19
00:03:10,050 --> 00:03:19,550
So: report repointed in place, and sixteen of sixteen visuals match. The code, the mapping, and the parity results are in the repo.
